Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform production-grade certificate rotation with minimal risk, including detection, canary strategy, and rollback.

Key Concepts

- cert-manager rotation and Issuer/Secret management
- mTLS handshakes and trust chain validation
- canary deployments and controlled rollouts
- observability for certificate issues (logs, events, cert status)
- rollback plans and verification

Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js sketch to trigger rotation and verify (simplified)
const {execSync} = require('child_process');
try {
  // Trigger rotation by refreshing CA secret (placeholder)
  execSync('kubectl delete secret cacerts -n istio-system');
  // Wait for new certs to be issued and pods to reconcile
  console.log('Rotation initiated, awaiting reconciliation...');
} catch (e) {
  console.error(e);
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify end-to-end trust after rotation (test client, gateway logs, and sni resolution)?
- How would you automate this process for future rotations with zero-downtime guarantees?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about Kubernetes resources and Spark on Kubernetes, and perform practical memory tuning with metrics, GC, and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes resource requests/limits
- Spark memory tuning (driver/executor memory, memoryOverhead, spark.memory.fraction)
- JVM GC options and GC logging
- Shuffle-heavy workloads and GC pressure
- Observability (Spark UI, kubectl top, metrics)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kubernetes patch example (pseudo)
const patch = {
  spec: {
    template: {
      spec: {
        containers: [
          { name: "executor",
            resources: { requests: { memory: "12Gi" }, limits: { memory: "12Gi" } }
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect a memory leak vs normal GC pauses?
- How would you verify stability after changes with a canary run?
